-# Bento Etal 2023 JEEM
+# Bento, Miller, Mookerjee, and Severnini (2023) JEEM
 
-**Summary**: Bento, Miller, Mookerjee, and Severnini introduce a unifying empirical method that decomposes daily temperature into a 30-year monthly moving-average climate norm and a deviation-from-norm weather shock, then estimates the short-run and long-run responses jointly in a single panel fixed-effects equation via the Frisch–Waugh–Lovell theorem (source: Bento_Miller_Mookerjee_Severnini_2023_JEEM.pdf). The norm-shock gap is the empirical measure of adaptation, and this design is the methodological backbone for the JMP's norm-shock decomposition of ozone (source: Bento_Miller_Mookerjee_Severnini_2023_JEEM.pdf).
+**Summary**: Proposes a unifying regression framework — exploiting the Frisch–Waugh–Lovell theorem — that estimates short-run weather-shock effects and long-run climate-norm effects in a single equation, directly recovering an adaptation measure as their difference. Applied to ambient ozone in the U.S. 1980–2013, finds adaptation of 0.51 ppb/°C and shows ignoring it overestimates the "climate penalty" by ~44%.
 
 **Sources**: Bento_Miller_Mookerjee_Severnini_2023_JEEM.pdf
 
