-## Project-Relevant Takeaway
+## Bibliographic information
 
-Bento et al. show how to decompose a daily meteorological variable into a long-run norm and a contemporaneous shock and then identify both responses in the same panel fixed-effects regression (source: Bento_Miller_Mookerjee_Severnini_2023_JEEM.pdf). The JMP inherits this design directly: it decomposes daily ozone $\rho_{it} = P_{im} + \epsilon_{it}$, treats $P_{im}$ as a backward-looking moving average (county × calendar month, lagged ≥1 year), and runs a single regression of health outcomes on both $P_{im}$ and the shock $\epsilon_{it}$ (source: Bento_Miller_Mookerjee_Severnini_2023_JEEM.pdf). The Frisch–Waugh–Lovell logic guarantees that $\beta^S$ on the shock identifies the same object as a standard fixed-effects regression of health on raw ozone, while $\beta^N$ on the norm identifies an adaptation-inclusive long-run response that is, in this design, free of the cross-sectional omitted-variable concerns that haunt pure Ricardian regressions (source: Bento_Miller_Mookerjee_Severnini_2023_JEEM.pdf).
+- **DOI**: 10.1016/j.jeem.2023.102843
+- **Replication archive**: https://doi.org/10.3886/E192708V1 (openICPSR)
+- **JEL codes**: Q53, Q54, C51
 
-## The Unifying Approach
+## Core contribution
 
-The ideal-but-infeasible equation embedding both short- and long-run responses is:
+The paper unifies two strands of the climate-economy literature: cross-sectional comparisons across climate zones and panel fixed-effects regressions exploiting day-to-day weather variation. The key insight is that both are embedded in a single regression via the [[frisch-waugh-lovell-decomposition]]:
 
 $$
-y_{it} = \alpha + \beta_W(x_{it} - \bar{x}_i) + \beta_C \bar{x}_i + \mu_i + \lambda_t + \nu_{it},
+y_{it} = \alpha + \beta_W(x_{it} - \bar{x}_{i\bar{p}}) + \beta_C \bar{x}_{i\bar{p}} + \mu_i + \lambda_s + \nu_{it}
 $$
 
-but $\bar{x}_i$ is collinear with the unit fixed effect $\mu_i$ (source: Bento_Miller_Mookerjee_Severnini_2023_JEEM.pdf). The feasible approximation replaces the strictly-contemporaneous $\bar{x}_i$ with a lagged historical average $\bar{x}_{i\bar{p}}$ and replaces $\lambda_t$ with $\lambda_s$ at one coarser time aggregation:
+- $\hat{\beta}_W$ recovers the panel FE estimate (no time for adaptation)
+- $\hat{\beta}_C$ recovers the cross-sectional estimate (full adaptation absorbed)
+- $\hat{\beta}_W - \hat{\beta}_C$ = the adaptation measure, testable with standard errors from this single regression
 
-$$
-y_{it} = \alpha + \beta_W(x_{it} - \bar{x}_{i\bar{p}}) + \beta_C \bar{x}_{i\bar{p}} + \mu_i + \lambda_s + \nu_{it}.
-$$
+See [[climate-norm-weather-shock-decomposition]] for the implementation details.
 
-Two sources of variation in $\bar{x}_{i\bar{p}}$ survive the unit fixed effect: (i) the year-to-year "update" of the moving average as the oldest year drops and the newest enters, and (ii) within-season variation across months of the same season-year (source: Bento_Miller_Mookerjee_Severnini_2023_JEEM.pdf).
+## Empirical application: ambient ozone
 
-In the empirical application to ambient ozone over U.S. counties 1980–2013, the norm is constructed as a 30-year monthly MA lagged by one year:
+Temperature affects ozone formation via photochemical reactions with NOx and VOCs. The paper estimates:
 
-$$
-\bar{x}_{i\bar{p}} = \frac{1}{30} \sum_{j=y-30}^{y-1} \bar{x}_{imj},
-$$
+| Parameter | Estimate | SE |
+|-----------|----------|----|
+| $\hat{\beta}_W$ (temperature shock) | 1.678 ppb/°C | 0.063*** |
+| $\hat{\beta}_C$ (climate norm, 30-yr MA) | 1.164 ppb/°C | 0.051*** |
+| Adaptation ($\hat{\beta}_W - \hat{\beta}_C$) | 0.514 ppb/°C | 0.041*** |
 
-and the estimating equation is:
+Panel FE benchmark: 1.659 ppb (0.063), statistically identical to $\hat{\beta}_W$ ✓  
+Cross-section benchmark: 1.166 ppb (0.106), statistically identical to $\hat{\beta}_C$ ✓
 
-$$
-Ozone_{it} = \beta_W \, Temp_{it}^W + \beta_C \, Temp_{i\bar{p}}^C + X_{it}'\delta + \phi_{is} + \epsilon_{it},
-$$
+## Climate penalty projections
 
-with $\phi_{is}$ monitor-by-season-by-year fixed effects, $s$ = Spring or Summer of the sample, and $X_{it}$ containing a similar norm/shock decomposition of precipitation (source: Bento_Miller_Mookerjee_Severnini_2023_JEEM.pdf). Standard errors are clustered at the county level.
+Using the norm-based $\hat{\beta}_C = 1.164$ (inclusive of adaptation) vs. the shock-based $\hat{\beta}_W = 1.678$ (exclusive of adaptation) under RCP 8.5 (1.6°C by 2050, 4.8°C by 2100):
 
-## Why Frisch–Waugh–Lovell
+| Scenario | Correct (norm) | Incorrect (shock) | Overestimate |
+|----------|---------------|-------------------|--------------|
+| 2050 | 1.9 ppb | 2.7 ppb | +0.82 ppb (+44%) |
+| 2100 | 5.6 ppb | 8.0 ppb | +2.47 ppb (+44%) |
 
-The FWL theorem (Lovell 1963, Theorem 4.1) is what makes the decomposition self-contained. Because the shock $(x_{it} - \bar{x}_{i\bar{p}})$ has been de-seasonalized in a first step (by subtracting a time-mean within unit), the shock coefficient $\hat{\beta}_W$ recovers the same object as a standard fixed-effects coefficient $\hat{\beta}_{FE}$ on raw $x_{it}$ in a separate regression (source: Bento_Miller_Mookerjee_Severnini_2023_JEEM.pdf). The key practical implication is that the outcome variable need not be de-seasonalized in the same way: Lovell's point 4 says it is immaterial whether the dependent variable is adjusted, provided the explanatory variables have been seasonally corrected (source: Bento_Miller_Mookerjee_Severnini_2023_JEEM.pdf). This is what allows both $\beta_W$ and $\beta_C$ to be estimated in the same equation rather than via SUR or block bootstrap across two separate equations.
+## Ozone Action Day null result
 
-In the paper's Table 1, this equivalence shows up concretely: column (1) reports the joint estimates ($\hat{\beta}_W = 1.678$, $\hat{\beta}_C = 1.164$), column (2) reports a standard fixed-effects regression of ozone on raw temperature ($\hat{\beta}_{FE} = 1.659$), and column (3) reports a cross-sectional regression on monitor-averaged temperature ($\hat{\beta}_{CS} = 1.166$) (source: Bento_Miller_Mookerjee_Severnini_2023_JEEM.pdf). The shock coefficient in column (1) is statistically equivalent to the fixed-effects coefficient in column (2), and the norm coefficient is statistically equivalent to the cross-sectional coefficient — but identified jointly within the panel equation rather than from a separate regression on a 2,712-observation cross-section.
+Interacting a binary OAD alert indicator with the temperature shock tests for short-run adaptive responses:
 
-## Adaptation as the Norm–Shock Gap
+$$
+\text{Shock} \times \text{Action Day}: \quad \hat{\beta} = 0.068, \quad \text{SE} = 0.188 \quad (\text{insignificant})
+$$
 
-Adaptation in this framework is defined as the difference $\hat{\beta}_W - \hat{\beta}_C$ — what would have happened to ozone for a 1°C change in raw temperature absent any behavioral response, minus what actually happens to ozone under a 1°C shift in the long-run norm (source: Bento_Miller_Mookerjee_Severnini_2023_JEEM.pdf). The Lucas-style intuition is that transitory shocks leave agents only hours to respond, so $\hat{\beta}_W$ is approximately the no-adaptation response; long-run norms are anticipated and let agents adjust production schedules, technology, and emissions decisions, so $\hat{\beta}_C$ is the adaptation-inclusive response (source: Bento_Miller_Mookerjee_Severnini_2023_JEEM.pdf).
+No meaningful short-run adaptive response detected nationally. This is consistent with alerts being insufficient to trigger behavioral change at the aggregate level — a finding directly relevant to the JMP project's test of whether alert programs induce additional defensive action. See [[forecasts-alerts-and-identification]].
 
-For the JMP, the sign convention is flipped because the relevant outcome (health) responds positively to ambient pollution, and ex-ante defensive action is what *closes* the gap. The JMP gap $\beta^S - \beta^N$ is, by analogy, the net health effect of agents' ex-ante defensive stock $k$, accounting for any rational crowding-out of contemporaneous action $b$ by $k$ in the health-production function. See [[norm-shock-decomposition]] and [[ex-ante-ex-post-adaptation]].
-
-## Headline Findings
-
-A 1°C temperature shock raises daily maximum ambient ozone by 1.68 ppb. A 1°C shift in the 30-year norm raises ozone by 1.16 ppb. The norm-shock gap — the measure of adaptation — is 0.51 ppb per °C (SE 0.041), highly significant (source: Bento_Miller_Mookerjee_Severnini_2023_JEEM.pdf). Using RCP 8.5 projections (Vose et al. 2017) of 1.6°C by 2050 and 4.8°C by 2100, the implied climate penalty on ozone is 1.9 ppb (2050) and 5.6 ppb (2100); using only the shock coefficient (the no-adaptation counterfactual) would over-predict the penalty by ~44% (2.7 and 8 ppb) (source: Bento_Miller_Mookerjee_Severnini_2023_JEEM.pdf).
-
-A short-run adaptation check using Ozone Action Day alerts (2004–2013 subsample) interacts the shock with an OAD indicator. The interaction is 0.068 (SE 0.188), statistically zero; the authors interpret this as evidence that short-run adaptation is small relative to long-run adaptation (source: Bento_Miller_Mookerjee_Severnini_2023_JEEM.pdf). This is the closest direct test of alert-program effects in the paper, and it is one of the JMP's motivations: refine the design to detect alert-induced behavioral responses in *health* outcomes rather than ozone formation.
-
 ## Robustness
 
-Robustness across moving-average windows (3, 5, 10, 20, 30 years) yields very stable estimates: $\hat{\beta}_W \in [1.669, 1.678]$, $\hat{\beta}_C \in [1.158, 1.176]$, adaptation $\in [0.495, 0.514]$ (source: Bento_Miller_Mookerjee_Severnini_2023_JEEM.pdf). 10-year and 20-year MA *lags* (rather than 1-year) yield slightly larger adaptation (0.527, 0.542), consistent with more time to adjust (source: Bento_Miller_Mookerjee_Severnini_2023_JEEM.pdf). Daily-vs.-monthly MAs, monthly-aggregated data, wind-speed and sunlight controls, balanced-vs.-unbalanced panels, exclusion of ozone-precursor-policy regions, alternative weather-station matching algorithms, and state-level vs. county-level clustering all yield qualitatively similar central estimates (source: Bento_Miller_Mookerjee_Severnini_2023_JEEM.pdf).
+Adaptation estimate is stable across MA lengths (3–30 years): range 0.495–0.542 ppb/°C. Longer lag structures (10- and 20-year lags) yield 0.527–0.542 ppb/°C. See Table 2 in `_text.md`.
 
-The norm-coefficient's stability across MA windows is itself informative: the standard Solon-style measurement-error argument says longer windows should reduce attenuation bias, but the slight movement of $\hat{\beta}_C$ across windows is consistent with two competing forces — measurement-error reduction (longer is better) and fixed-effects-induced attenuation (Blanc and Schlenker 2017 — longer makes the demeaned variation a larger share of total variation). 20–30-year windows appear near the optimum for the bias trade-off in this setting (source: Bento_Miller_Mookerjee_Severnini_2023_JEEM.pdf).
+## Data and sample
 
-## Nonlinear Extension
+- Ozone: EPA AQS, daily max concentration, monitor × day, 1980–2013, ozone season (April–September)
+- Temperature: NOAA GHCN, 20,000+ stations; matched to ozone monitors within 30 km
+- N = 5,139,523 monitor-day observations; 2,712 monitors in cross-section
+- Unit of observation: ozone monitor × day (note: JMP project uses county-level data)
 
-The linear specification can be extended to quadratic or higher-order polynomials or to a 5°C-binned linear specification (source: Bento_Miller_Mookerjee_Severnini_2023_JEEM.pdf). The marginal adaptation in a quadratic model is $(\beta_W - \beta_C) + 2(\beta_{W2}(x_{it}-\bar{x}_{i\bar{p}}) - \beta_{C2}\bar{x}_{i\bar{p}})$ (source: Bento_Miller_Mookerjee_Severnini_2023_JEEM.pdf). Empirically, the binned and cubic specifications produce an "inverted-U" marginal effect (ozone rises at a decreasing rate at high temperatures) and a "normal-U" marginal adaptation (adaptation is larger when temperature is hotter), suggesting agents focus adaptive effort on the days that would otherwise produce the most ozone (source: Bento_Miller_Mookerjee_Severnini_2023_JEEM.pdf).
