-## Implications for the JMP
-
-The JMP applies the Bento-Miller-Mookerjee-Severnini decomposition directly to ozone exposure rather than to temperature. The pollution norm $P_{im}$ is constructed as a backward-looking moving average of daily ozone at the county × calendar-month level, lagged by at least one year. The shock is $\epsilon_{it} = \rho_{it} - P_{im}$. Health is regressed jointly on both in a single panel equation:
-
-$$
-h_{it} = \beta^S (\rho_{it} - P_{im}) + \beta^N P_{im} + \beta_A^S (\rho_{it}-P_{im}) A_{it} + \beta_A^N P_{im} A_{it} + \beta_A A_{it} + X'_{it}\gamma + \alpha_{is} + \delta_{ky} + \nu_{it},
-$$
-
-where $A_{it}$ is the alert indicator, $\alpha_{is}$ is county-by-season FE, and $\delta_{ky}$ is state-by-year FE (source: Bento_Miller_Mookerjee_Severnini_2023_JEEM.pdf as methodological template).
-
-Key differences and choices: (i) the JMP's MA window is 5 years, not 30, because 5 years is closer to the timescale over which agents can plausibly update beliefs about pollution norms and adjust ex-ante defensive stock $k$; (ii) the JMP's unit is county-day rather than monitor-day; (iii) the JMP adds alert interactions to test whether alert programs shift the norm and shock coefficients; (iv) the JMP wind-direction-IVs both the shock and the norm separately, following Deryugina et al. (2019) for the daily shock and using historical monthly wind-direction shares for the norm. See [[norm-shock-decomposition]] and [[adapting-wind-iv-to-ozone-shocks]].
